omx-contract (2.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New controls. The delay's ping-pong switch, which comes up at
    FX_DELAY_PINGPONG_DEFAULT. The drive's auto-gain and stereo-link
    switches and its HF roll-off corner (off, 12 kHz, 16 kHz). The
    compressor's kind (comp or limiter, which picks the RMS or peak
    detector) and its detector oversampling (auto, off, 4x).
  * The EQ kernel now lists the controls an EQ instance takes, in the
    instance's order: the HPF and LPF switches, corners and slopes, then the
    per-band type, frequency, gain, Q and switch. The per-band controls name
    EQ_BAND_COUNTS as their count, and the graphic EQ's band names
    GEQ_BANDS. The notch's wider Q travel is listed as the Q control's
    travel while the band is a notch.
  * A control may take the item another control of its kernel declares
    (the LPF slope uses FILTER_SLOPES), and a choice's default may refer to
    the scalar that states it. Every define of the 2.1.0 C header is
    unchanged; the new sets are added.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:09:24 +0000

omx-contract (2.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The JSON render lists each kernel's controls in their declared order,
    with the kind, the global each one lives in and the table of a table
    field, so a consumer reads control order and kinds from the render
    instead of the kernel files. The existing JSON keys and the C and
    TypeScript renders are unchanged.
  * A control may declare "rearms": changing it re-arms the kernel's state,
    so it is not a smooth parameter. The limiter's look-ahead declares it.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0000

omx-contract (2.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A kernel file now holds four sections: its controls (each a travel or a
    choice, in order), the tables some of them are fields of, the aggregates
    that list controls by name, and its constants. A control's item name
    follows from the kernel and its own name unless it says otherwise. Every
    render is the same: the C headers byte for byte, the JSON item for item,
    the TypeScript declaration for declaration. A kernel file written the old
    way, as a flat map of items, is refused by `omx-contract validate`, and
    the kernel wizard writes the new shape.
  * The band_dyn kernel: the travels of a multiband dynamics band, read from
    the de-esser and the compressor where they declare them, its modes, its
    knee and its band count, plus the shortest attack at which the
    compressor's detector does not alias, the floor of the band's attack
    travel.
  * HRP_AMOUNT_RANGE comes up from the desk, like every other travel.
  * `omx-contract semver` against a release before 2.0.0 reads that release's
    kernel files in the new shape, so it reports only the items that changed.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

omx-contract (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Corrects the 1.2.0 note: omx-dsp could not yet build against the header
    rendered here, because 38 limits its code reads were missing from it (the
    feedback detector, the ring correction, the RTA, the bus input cap and the
    EQ band reserve). They are all here now, with the value omx-dsp uses
    today, so the list of limits omx-contract lacks that omx-dsp keeps ends
    empty. The three EQ band budget numbers that 1.2.0 said stay in openmixer
    are in too: the budget is declared here and `omx-contract validate`
    refuses data where the operator's reserve plus the feedback and ring
    planters' bands no longer fit the EQ's maximum.
  * A new kind of item, the set: ordered ids with an optional default and
    labels. C gets an enum with a `_COUNT` and, where there is a default, a
    `_DEFAULT`; TypeScript gets the list of ids; the JSON render carries the
    default and the labels. A travel can now say how a host draws it (`scale`,
    one of `PARAM_SCALES`: linear, log or stepped), a derivation `ceilSum`
    rounds a sum up, and an item outside the kernels can record the release
    that added it (`since`).
  * The fbs kernel: 20 values the feedback detector reads, from the guard
    bands and the growth gates to the default thresholds and the notch policy,
    read from the engine where it declares them.
  * The hrp kernel: 9 values the ring correction reads, read from the engine
    where it declares them, plus its amount travel with the come-up value 0.5
    omx-dsp uses.
  * The rta kernel: 2 values, read from the engine where it declares them: the
    target bin width and the largest FFT.
  * The mixmatrix kernel: 1 value, read from the engine where it declares it:
    the input cap of a summing bus.
  * The eq kernel: 8 more values. The band types and the pass-filter slopes as
    sets, with the labels a host shows. The operator's band reserve. How many
    bands each strip type starts with and may hold (`channel` 4 of 24, `strip`
    4, `eq8` 8, `eq16` 16, `eq32` 32): a start never exceeds the maximum, and
    the default centres of any count come from one rule for every strip, host
    and consumer, with no override. The rule: four bands start at 100, 400,
    2000 and 8000 Hz; any other count is spread evenly on a log axis across
    the band frequency range and snapped to the ISO 266 R10 preferred
    frequencies (to R20 where R10 is too coarse to give each band its own
    centre, as at 32 bands); the first band is a low shelf and the last a high
    shelf, each switchable to a bell, and every band between is a bell. Every
    band comes up flat at Q 1, the value omx-plugins ship for bells and
    shelves, so no shelf Q is invented. The pass filters come up at 80 Hz and
    18 kHz. `eqDefaultCentres`, `eqDefaultTypes` and `eqDefaultBands` in the
    TypeScript module and `omx_eq_default_centres` and `omx_eq_default_type`
    in the C header run the rule, and a test holds the three equal for every
    count. The console's previous starter bands (a low shelf at 120 Hz, bells
    at 800 and 3000 Hz, a high shelf at 10 kHz, Q 0.7 and 1) change to these;
    gains come up at 0 dB, so nothing sounds different.
  * The EQ's three frequency travels, as a host draws them, in
    `EQ_CONTINUOUS_TRAVELS`: the band frequency and the two pass-filter
    corners with no step, on a log scale.
  * The deesser kernel: 1 more value, the de-esser's modes, as a set.
  * The drive kernel: 2 more values, the curves and the bands, as sets.
  * The reverb kernel: 2 more values, the algorithms as a set and the gated
    reverb's gate attack.
  * The rotary kernel: 1 more value, the speeds, as a set.
  * The tremolo kernel: 1 more value, the modes, as a set coming up as
    tremolo.
  * The gate kernel: 1 more value, the key sources (self or sidechain), as a
    set.
  * The chorus kernel: 2 more values, the base delay and the longest delay a
    voice reaches (the base plus the depth maximum, rounded up).
  * The flanger kernel: 2 more values, likewise.
  * The phaser kernel: 1 more value, the top frequency of the sweep.
  * Two measurement rate sets for the oracles: the four every kernel runs at
    (`ORACLE_FLOOR_RATES`) and the nine an RME interface clocks at
    (`RME_RATES`).
  * The ISO third-octave centres now have a C spelling
    (`OMX_ISO_THIRD_OCTAVE_CENTRES_HZ_INIT`).
  * The leftover parameter-header renderer of the retired plugin data is gone,
    with the frozen delay header it was proved against: omx-plugins renders
    its own parameter headers.
  * The kernel wizard cites an item the engine does not declare by an `origin`
    sentence (the omx-dsp header or the ruling that first declares it) and
    takes a doc for a value that shares its comment, and the importer resolves
    `@openmixer/omx-contract` from the engine's installed copy.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 12:01:00 +0000

